Not one “kink center,” and usually not just a crossed wire.

Sexual attraction/arousal is distributed across networks involving reward, salience, memory, emotion, attention, body-state, and valuation—regions such as the amygdala, hypothalamus, striatum, insula, anterior cingulate, and prefrontal/orbitofrontal cortex all show up in imaging work. 

A kink or fetish can emerge from several routes at once:

baseline preference / temperament
+ associative learning
+ novelty
+ reward
+ repeated pairing
+ taboo or salience
+ imagination
+ autobiographical experience

Sexual cues can become conditioned: something initially neutral can acquire arousing value if it repeatedly predicts sexual reward. That is established in both animal work and, more cautiously, in humans. 

So “crossed wires” is sometimes a decent metaphor for two circuits becoming associated, but it implies malfunction when there may be none. Most unusual consensual sexual interests are not considered mental disorders merely because they are unusual; clinically, distress, impairment, or harm/non-consent are the important distinctions. 

Your pink-elephant analogy is related but separate. Trying not to think about something can sometimes increase monitoring for that thought:

“do not think X”
→ brain keeps checking “am I thinking X?”
→ X repeatedly reactivated

The classic literature calls that ironic thought suppression or rebound. But newer reviews make the picture more nuanced: rebound is not inevitable, and suppression can work in some people and contexts. 

With attraction, this can create a nasty loop:

notice unwanted thought
→ alarm: why did I think that?
→ monitor for it
→ notice it more
→ interpret frequency as desire
→ more alarm

Crucially:

thought ≠ attraction ≠ desire ≠ intention ≠ identity.

Sometimes people accidentally strengthen a mental association by repeatedly checking whether it is gone.

The coding analogy is pretty good. Imagine a classifier with a feedback loop. If you tell it:

IF stimulus X appears, urgently check whether X matters

you have increased X’s salience even while trying to suppress it.

A better algorithm is often:

detect X
→ don't catastrophize
→ don't compulsively test
→ redirect toward chosen target
→ reinforce desired associations
→ let unused pathway lose salience

For unwanted but harmless thoughts, that often means treating them as noise rather than conducting an interrogation every time they appear.

If someone has an unwanted sexual interest that genuinely causes distress, compulsive behavior, or involves non-consenting/illegal targets, that becomes a different problem and is worth working through with a qualified clinician. But for ordinary odd or unwanted associations, the first move usually should not be “uncross the wire by force.”

It is often closer to:

stop feeding the monitoring loop, understand what reinforced the association, and strengthen the channels you actually want.

That fits the broader pattern we’ve been talking about: you often change a system more effectively by rerouting flow than by attacking the old channel head-on.